The Mesa project began as an open-source implementation of the OpenGL
specification - a system for rendering interactive 3D graphics. Over the 
years the project has grown to implement more graphics APIs, including 
OpenCL, OpenMAX, VDPAU, VA API, XvMC and Vulkan.

A variety of device drivers allows the Mesa libraries to be used in many
different environments ranging from software emulation to complete
hardware acceleration for modern GPUs.

Mesa ties into several other open-source projects: the Direct Rendering
Infrastructure and X.org to provide OpenGL support on Linux, FreeBSD
and other operating systems.

This package containing the core libraries must be installed on any system 
using Mesa. Driver subpackages (:dri_gl_intel, :dri_gl_amd, etc.) provide 
hardware-specific DRI modules and should be installed based on your GPU 
vendor. For software rendering fallback, install the :dri_gl_swrast 
subpackage.

Note: OpenGL ES and EGL support have been omitted as these APIs are 
deprecated in Mesa. Vulkan and Wayland support is currently disabled.
